1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,360
Hello everyone, thanks for joining us. My name is Daph, I'm the creator of Verp Suite.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,040
I'm going to talk today about AI in pen testing, how we're approaching it, and the new features

3
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:24,480
that have just landed in Verp Suite. While I'm talking, please post any questions that you have.

4
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:32,480
The link for posting questions should be in the chat. At the end, I'll try to answer as many as I can.

5
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:46,000
So, why AI for Verp and why now? Like most of the world, application security is at an inflection point with AI.

6
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:53,600
Applications are getting more complex, and so pen testing them effectively is getting more complex as well.

7
00:00:55,280 --> 00:01:04,880
AI can handle a lot of grunt work, large volumes of data. It can automate more of the repetitive or the

8
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:13,520
mundane work than previous generations of automation were able to, leaving human testers to focus on the

9
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:23,200
critical stuff. Attackers have access to AI too. They can scale attacks and move faster than ever.

10
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:32,000
That means defenders can't afford to fall behind. Pen testers really need to take advantage of the

11
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,840
efficiency and scalability that AI provides.

12
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:44,240
Now, pen testers tend to be a skeptical bunch, and I come from the pen testing world as well.

13
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:49,040
Pen testers tend to be a little bit more complex.

14
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:53,040
I'm not sure if you're a little bit more complex, but I'm not sure if you're a little bit more complex,

15
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:55,040
but I'm not sure if you're a little bit more complex.

16
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:57,040
I'm not sure if you're a little bit more complex, but I'm not sure if you're a little bit more complex.

17
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:02,400
Just to say, hey, look, Burp's got AI. I'm pretty allergic to marketing fluff.

18
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:09,680
So it's vital for us that anything we put into Burp has to have substance, has to deliver

19
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,840
real value, has to be features that people actually want to use.

20
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:20,480
We're also pretty skeptical about any kind of black box magic.

21
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:25,520
So, hey, we've made a robo hacker that can do what skilled pen testers can do.

22
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:31,040
I wouldn't trust anything claims to do that because it certainly can't.

23
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:37,120
We're interested in using AI within your existing workflows in a way

24
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:40,880
that you can see what it's doing and you can work with it.

25
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:46,720
We're also very mindful of concerns about data handling.

26
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:52,160
So some people are doing crazy things, throwing sensitive data around, training on it,

27
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:57,040
piping untrusted input into powerful agents to perform actions.

28
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:02,880
We're security people. We're instinctively very cautious about that.

29
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:08,240
So if you're skeptical as well, we totally understand that.

30
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:12,480
We assume you will be skeptical until you see AI in action in Burp.

31
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:18,160
What we hope is that when you do see it in action, you will be as excited as we are

32
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:22,720
and that we'll earn your trust that we're going about this in the right way.

33
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:31,520
Our core philosophy is that automation is about augmenting human testers,

34
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:32,880
not replacing them.

35
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:40,800
When I first created Burp Suite more than 20 years ago, it was basically because I was a lazy pen

36
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:48,960
tester. I was doing a lot of boring, repetitive stuff, the same thing over and over, 100 times on

37
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:52,080
every test and then the same thing again on the next test and the next test after that.

38
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:58,960
I used to write Perl scripts to scale things up, send inputs to different parameters, cycle through

39
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:06,000
number ranges, things like that. That led to Burp Intruder so that I could set those up, set those

40
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:14,240
attacks up faster and more easily. Basically, the whole of Burp Suite's feature set over the following

41
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:20,080
years has been driven by that same philosophy, which is find the things that can be offloaded to the

42
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:28,560
machine and put those at the fingertips of the user. People often say Burp is the Swiss army knife

43
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:35,360
of pen testing. It's very much down to you which tools you choose for a particular task.

44
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:46,000
We approached AI with exactly that same philosophy. So we asked how can we use AI to make even better

45
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:53,760
power tools for pen testers to use in their workflows? Even with the very best classical

46
00:04:53,760 --> 00:05:00,480
automation, there are still lots of repetitive things that people have to do. Because those tasks

47
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:06,720
need intelligence, they need an actual understanding of what's going on, how an application function is

48
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:13,840
supposed to work, what it's for, and the kind of things that might go wrong with it. What AI offers is

49
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:20,480
a generational leap in the kind of things that machines that can reliably, machines can reliably do.

50
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:27,280
So there's now a ton more things, quite boring, repetitive tasks that you can offload to the

51
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:35,120
automation, just like I did with Burp Intruder 20 years ago. You're still in control, you decide what

52
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:42,800
to offload. You basically have more options for where you focus your time and attention for the most

53
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:47,920
creative and critical tasks. So you can focus on the things that do need your expertise,

54
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:52,560
and that actually are the most fun and the most rewarding things to do yourself.

55
00:05:55,120 --> 00:06:04,800
So with that philosophy in mind, how did we go about building AI? Like everyone else in 2023, our minds

56
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:14,000
were blown by what LLMs were capable of. We formed a small team working on the question, how could AI deliver

57
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:22,960
real value to pen testers? How could AI do for today's workflows what Burp Intruder did previously?

58
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:31,200
That team went broad. They came up with tons of ideas. Some great, some were crazy,

59
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:39,200
somewhat complete duds. They had quite an emotional roller coaster of optimism and despair, which was

60
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:48,160
funny to watch. Then later in 2024, we brought in more of our engineers. We had a bunch of AI hack weeks

61
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:53,840
where people played with some of the ideas, they added some new ones, and they figured out what could work.

62
00:06:53,840 --> 00:07:02,080
We also began building out all the infrastructure that we'd need to deliver AI capabilities at scale

63
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:08,960
and creating a really robust testing ecosystem where we could road test different approaches with

64
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:17,440
different models and measure how well they performed. Technology was obviously moving pace around us,

65
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:22,240
so being able to quickly measure the progress in the technology and the progress that we were making

66
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:29,680
was vital. Towards the end of last year, we had a bunch of features working reasonably well,

67
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:34,240
and it was time to get these into the hands of users to see what they thought.

68
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:41,040
We then had a private beta with around 30 experienced Burp users.

69
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:45,840
We had a private beta users, and this was a real crunch moment, finding out if the features really

70
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:52,640
were valuable in the field, surfacing any concerns people had, seeing if our infrastructure could handle

71
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:53,760
real world usage at scale.

72
00:07:53,760 --> 00:08:04,000
The feedback we got from the beta users was absolute gold. So they loved some things, they hated others,

73
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:10,000
they definitely weren't shy about telling us what they thought. There were some surprises where a particular

74
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:19,360
feature was way less useful or way more useful than we'd expected. We identified situations where the AI was

75
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:27,680
over-extended. Someone said it was like an eager junior pen tester, mostly helpful, but sometimes definitely

76
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:35,920
overconfident. That let us refine a bunch of things that was either to rein in what the AI was doing or to

77
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:42,400
push it further. We made some of the features more transparent so you could see more of how they worked

78
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:44,640
to get more trust that they were doing the right thing.

79
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:52,720
We absolutely stuck to our core philosophy. This had to be about valuable features that people

80
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:57,520
actually wanted to use and would trust. Definitely no gimmicks or black boxes.

81
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:05,280
And that brings us to today. So we've just launched this week the first set of five new features

82
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:11,360
for UpSuite to everyone. I'm not going to demo anything today, I think that'll be far too risky, but

83
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:19,520
I'll briefly describe each of those features. So the first is explore issue. So you can think of this

84
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:27,520
as an assistant that picks up where the scanner leaves off. When the scanner finds a potential issue,

85
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:35,520
you can use explore issue to dig deeper, validate the issue, demonstrate the impact, look for ways to

86
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:42,880
escalate it. It's like having an extra set of eyes that never gets tired of investigating findings.

87
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:50,800
So you can do things like generate proof of concepts, proof of concept exploits instantly, save you time.

88
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:57,680
It can uncover extra insights like leaked data or escalation paths, things you might, you might have missed.

89
00:09:58,960 --> 00:10:06,000
It's great for gathering evidence you can use in pen test reports. And the key thing is that you stay

90
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:11,520
completely in control. So you decide when you set it running, you can see every step that it's taking,

91
00:10:11,520 --> 00:10:15,920
you can follow its reasoning, what it's tried, what it's found, what didn't work.

92
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:22,080
You can intervene at any time, pick things up yourself manually, or just tell it to stop.

93
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:30,640
This feature was an absolute smash hit with our beta testers. For some issues like file disclosure

94
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:37,360
vulnerabilities, they would have spent a lot of time following up to go hunting the useful files.

95
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:42,160
Things like configuring intruder attacks, finding the right word lists, digging through the results.

96
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:48,480
You might be talking an hour or two of time to get the really the best proof of concept, the most

97
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:54,000
juicy disclosure. They found that explore issue did all of that in about five minutes.

98
00:10:56,880 --> 00:11:02,160
The second feature is explainer. So this is really a quality of life thing.

99
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:07,600
When you're testing, if you're like me, you continuously run into things that you aren't

100
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:16,000
familiar with. You might see an obscure HTTP header or some parameter that looks like it's part of a UI

101
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:22,400
framework. You might see some JavaScript code that you don't really have time to dig into.

102
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:31,680
When you find yourself asking, what's this? You can use explainer to ask exactly that. Just select the

103
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:39,680
thing and explainer will give you a quick security focused explanation. Today's models basically have

104
00:11:39,680 --> 00:11:47,360
all the knowledge that is out there. So it's like having a seasoned guru sitting next to you who can

105
00:11:47,360 --> 00:11:55,600
immediately give you context on what you're looking at. This feature isn't actually super intelligent,

106
00:11:55,600 --> 00:12:00,560
but it doesn't need to be. So we know from talking to users that many of you are routinely

107
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:07,680
pasting stuff into chat GPT and asking what's this explainer really just takes the grunt work out of

108
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:11,840
that. So you don't need to copy and paste. You don't need to alt tab. You don't need to write any prompts.

109
00:12:11,840 --> 00:12:19,520
It keeps you in the zone where you are. Like I said, we didn't do this as a particularly

110
00:12:19,520 --> 00:12:26,800
clever feature. It just does exactly what it says. What surprised us was how much our beta testers

111
00:12:26,800 --> 00:12:35,440
loved the feature. They said that AI has sometimes turned them into a chat GPT monkey doing, they're

112
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:39,920
now doing a new monotonous thing, which is copying and pasting stuff back and forth to us what it is.

113
00:12:39,920 --> 00:12:43,920
Explainer really just takes away that monkey work and inlines it for you.

114
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:53,520
The third feature is AI generated afforded logins. When you're testing an application with a login,

115
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:59,120
you need to configure the scanner to play well with that. You know the drill, you need to record a

116
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:05,200
login sequence so the scanner can authenticate reliably. Normally that means opening a browser,

117
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:11,440
clicking through login forms, dealing with single sign on or whatever. And that can be surprisingly

118
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:18,560
flaky. Sometimes you have to be really careful to get the steps precisely right so that Burps

119
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:23,600
automation can reproduce the login reliably. And that can take time, it can be frustrating.

120
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:31,680
With the new feature, you skip all of that. You just give Burp your credentials to the application

121
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:39,360
and it logs you in just like a human would. So in practice, it's a huge time saver and it gives you

122
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:45,920
better coverage in your test. If it's way easier to configure logins and the logins are reliable,

123
00:13:45,920 --> 00:13:53,360
then you're much less likely to miss blind spots. This was a feature that our BC users raved about.

124
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:57,920
You take an app that always gives you headaches, which is really painful to get the login working

125
00:13:57,920 --> 00:14:03,680
reliably. They said it just works. I don't know how it does it so much better than me, but I'm not

126
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:11,280
going back to setting it up myself. Now we can't promise that this will work perfectly every time,

127
00:14:11,280 --> 00:14:16,640
but in 90% or more of situations, it should just work straight away.

128
00:14:19,280 --> 00:14:26,160
The fourth feature is false positive reduction for access control bugs. False positives are obviously

129
00:14:26,960 --> 00:14:33,520
pain in the ass. Any kind of automated testing brings with it the potential for false positives.

130
00:14:33,520 --> 00:14:44,640
So we decided to tackle one of the trickiest categories of issue, which is broken access controls.

131
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:52,480
Testing for access control issues is still one of the areas that involves a lot of repetition and

132
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:58,880
monotony. A typical application might have dozens of functions that you need to test. There might be

133
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:03,760
different levels of privilege. You might have complex multi-step sequences where access controls can fail

134
00:15:03,760 --> 00:15:11,120
at any point. As a tester, you often get a big matrix of things that you need to test and working through

135
00:15:11,120 --> 00:15:19,200
all of those manually is really malicious. But it's also an area where automated tools in the past

136
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:25,440
haven't been able to do a very good job. This is something that we've worked on a lot over the years

137
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:33,520
of the past. Using classical automation, it's possible to fairly reliably detect when a function or an

138
00:15:33,520 --> 00:15:39,840
action can be accessed in a particular user context. So the automation can tell whether or not you need

139
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:45,760
to be logged in to access it and who you need to be logged in as. What it hasn't been able to do previously

140
00:15:45,760 --> 00:15:51,680
is tell you whether you should be able to access it as your current user. The machine couldn't tell the

141
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:57,920
difference between things that are sensitive and things that aren't. VerbScanner has had the ability

142
00:15:57,920 --> 00:16:05,600
to test access controls for a long time, but it's never been reliably enough for us to really recommend

143
00:16:05,600 --> 00:16:10,240
that people use it. And that's because of the limitations of that classical automation.

144
00:16:12,640 --> 00:16:19,280
AI has been an absolute game changer for this. It can understand the purpose of an application function.

145
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:26,400
It can say fairly reliably whether the user should be able to access it. So it can filter the output from

146
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:33,280
the classical automation and get rid of false positives. Essentially, it knows what real access

147
00:16:33,280 --> 00:16:40,320
control issues look like. The practical benefit is that it's a massive time saver on repetitive work.

148
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:46,560
You don't have to do everything by hand. You also don't have to dig through a ton of false positives either.

149
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:52,320
You can put more trust into the scanner, into the scanner output. So when it reports an access control

150
00:16:52,320 --> 00:16:58,320
issue, you can have more faith in it. That means you spend more of your scarce time on the most valuable

151
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:06,880
tasks. Now, this really is just the start for us of using AI to reduce noise and scanning. We picked

152
00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:12,400
access controls first because it's so prevalent and so painful to test for. We're going to extend this

153
00:17:12,400 --> 00:17:18,960
approach to more vulnerability classes. So we'll be continuously improving that signal to noise ratio.

154
00:17:21,520 --> 00:17:25,120
And the fifth feature is extensibility through the Montoya API.

155
00:17:25,120 --> 00:17:32,800
We know that you guys are incredibly innovative in writing Burp extensions. You come up with all kinds

